Subject: model with loading
Message-ID: <OZSPZUl576Q.Yk3CBvnnAAAJ@genomic-sem-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/genomic-sem-users/c/OZSPZUl576Q/m/Yk3CBvnnAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Sir

I have conducted GWAS by subtraction for six phenotypes using two different methods. For the first method, we employed the following formula:

model <- "IO=~NA*GCIPL + IOP

          NIO=~NA*GCIPL

          NIO~~1*NIO

          IO~~1*IO

          NIO~~0*IO

          IO~SNP

          NIO~SNP

          IOP ~~ 0*GCIPL

          IOP ~~ 0*IOP

          GCIPL ~~ 0*GCIPL

          SNP~~SNP"

In this case, no loading factors were used for phenotypes such as GCIPL or IOP, and 'NA' was assigned to them.

However, for phenotypes that exhibited a significant model without SNP, we employed the following approach:

model <- "IO=~0.07*GCIPL + (-1)*IOP

          NIO=~0.99*GCIPL

          NIO~~1*NIO

          IO~~1*IO

          NIO~~0*IO

          IO~SNP

          NIO~SNP

          IOP ~~ 0*GCIPL

          IOP ~~ 0*IOP

          GCIPL ~~ 0*GCIPL

          SNP~~SNP"

In this case, loading factors that was obtained from the analysis without SNP if it is significant  were applied to phenotypes like GCIPL and IOP. Notably, we observed that the p-values of the GWAS by subtraction then  became more significant, revealing a stronger relationship with our outcome.

I would appreciate it if you could confirm whether the approach we used is correct.

Thank you for your assistance.

Maryam

